    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-317312",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ff317cd2-0896-5134-b3a3-4e9f84e22b68",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant",
      "problem": "Let $a=⟨2,-1,3⟩$, $b=⟨0,4,1⟩$ and $c=⟨-2,1,5⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|\\,-\\,(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}|\\,+\\,3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\,(4\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 1)\\,-\\,(-1)\\,(0\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-2))\\,+\\,3\\,(0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2\\,(19)\\,-\\,(-1)\\,(2)\\,+\\,3\\,(8) \\\\  & =38+2+24 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
