    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-317316",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ff317cd2-0896-5134-b3a3-4e9f84e22b68",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Scalar Triple Product Using a Determinant",
      "problem": "Let $a=⟨0,2,5⟩$, $b=⟨3,1,0⟩$ and $c=⟨4,-1,-2⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|\\,-\\,2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}|\\,+\\,5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0\\,(1\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-1))\\,-\\,2\\,(3\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 4)\\,+\\,5\\,(3\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =0(-2)\\,-\\,2\\,(-6)\\,+\\,5\\,(-7) \\\\  & =0+12-35 \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
